.TH SVCTL 8 "September 2026" "Peios" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
svctl \- control Peios services, submitted jobs, and system shutdown
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B svctl
.RI [ options ]
.BR start | stop | restart | reload | reset | status
.I service
.br
.B svctl
.RI [ options ]
.BR list | reload-config | boot
.br
.B svctl
.RI [ options ]
.B op
.I operation-id
.br
.B svctl
.RI [ options ]
.B shutdown
.BR poweroff | reboot | halt
.br
.B svctl
.RI [ options ]
.B job
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B svctl
.RI [ --json ]
.B definition
.BR show | validate | create | edit | delete
.I service
.RI [ edits ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B svctl
is the administrative client for the Peios service manager.  Service and
system commands use the control socket.  Submitted-job commands use either
the control socket or the jobs socket, as described below.  Definition
commands read and write a service's definition in the registry, under
.IR Machine\eSystem\eServices .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --socket " path"
Use
.I path
instead of the default control socket.
.TP
.BI --jobs-socket " path"
Use
.I path
instead of the default submitted-jobs socket.
.TP
.B --json
Write machine-readable JSON output.
.TP
.BR --wait , " " --no-wait
Wait, or do not wait, for a supported asynchronous operation to complete.
.TP
.BR -h , " " --help
Show command usage.
.TP
.B --version
Show the client version.
.SH SERVICE COMMANDS
.TP
.BI start " service"
Start a service.
.TP
.BI stop " service"
Stop a service.
.TP
.BI restart " service"
Restart a service.
.TP
.BI reload " service"
Reload a service.
.TP
.BI reset " service"
Clear the service's failed state.
.TP
.BI status " service"
Show service status. Each calendar timer is shown under its schedule,
with its next occurrence, the time it fires when jitter delays that, and
when it last fired; or, if peinit did not arm it, why not. Times are UTC.
.TP
.B list
List services, with each service's soonest timer firing under
.BR "NEXT TIMER" .
.TP
.BI op " operation-id"
Show an operation.
.TP
.B reload-config
Reload service definitions from the registry.
.TP
.BI shutdown " poweroff|reboot|halt"
Request an immediate system shutdown of the selected kind.
.TP
.B boot
Show how this boot went: its mode
.RB ( full
or
.BR safe ),
why it is in that mode, how many boots before it started and never
counted as a success, against the number that sends the machine to
recovery, and whether this boot has counted yet.  A boot counts once
every Critical service has kept running for the grace period; until then
.B boot
names the Critical services it is waiting for, or, once they are all
running, when it will count.  Any signed-in user may ask, under the
default control descriptor.
.SH SUBMITTED JOBS
.TP
.B job list
List submitted jobs.  Results may be filtered with
.BR --submitter ,
.BR --identity ,
.BR --logon-session ,
or
.BR --state .
.TP
.BI "job status" " job-id"
Show one submitted job.
.TP
.BI "job stop" " job-id"
Stop one submitted job.
.TP
.BI "job submit" " [options] image [argument ...]"
Submit an absolute executable image.  Options include
.BR --description ,
.BR --cwd ,
repeatable
.BR --env ,
.BR --timeout ,
.BR --stop-timeout ,
.BR --readiness ,
.BR --readiness-timeout ,
repeatable
.BR --success-exit-code ,
repeatable
.BR --fd ,
.BR --output ,
and
.BR --security-descriptor .
The job runs with the caller's primary token.
.TP
.BI "job wait" " [--for ready|terminal] job-id"
Wait for a job to become ready or terminal.
.TP
.BI "job signal" " job-id signal"
Send a signal to a job.
.SH SERVICE DEFINITIONS
.B def
is short for
.BR definition .
Fields are named as in the registry, in any case.  A field is set from text:
a number as digits, yes or no, a choice by its name
.RB ( Type=Oneshot ),
and a list by giving
.B --set
once for each item, in order.  A change is checked as peinit checks a
definition, and nothing is written unless it passes.  Only the values that
change are written, in one registry transaction, and it is refused if any of
them changed since it was read.  Values that are not fields of a definition
are kept as they are.
.B ServiceSecurity
is not set here.
.TP
.BI "definition show" " service"
Show the fields that are set, by group, and whether peinit would take the
definition.
.TP
.BI "definition validate" " service"
Say whether peinit would take the definition, and if not, why.  Exits 1 if it
would not.  Privilege names, the services a definition names and its identity
are checked later, by peinit and authd, and are not checked here.
.TP
.BI "definition create" " service " "--set ImagePath=" "path " "[--set " field = value "]..."
Define a new service.
.TP
.BI "definition edit" " service " "[--set " field = value "]... [--unset " field ]...
Change a definition.  It says which of the changed fields a running service
keeps as they were until it is restarted.
.TP
.BI "definition delete" " service"
Delete a definition, and what is under its key.  A running service carries
on until it stops.
.SH MULTICALL NAMES
The installed
.BR reboot ,
.BR poweroff ,
.BR halt ,
and
.B shutdown
commands are links to
.BR svctl .
The first three request their named action immediately.
.B shutdown
accepts only immediate forms:
.BR "shutdown now" ,
.BR "shutdown -h now" ,
.BR "shutdown -P now" ,
.BR "shutdown -r now" ,
and
.BR "shutdown -H now" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success.  Command-usage errors return 64.  A waited submitted
job returns success only when the job completed successfully.  A definition
command returns 1 when peinit would not take the definition or the change
could not be written, and 69 when the registry could not be read.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/peinit/control.sock
Default service-control socket.
.TP
.I /run/peinit/jobs.sock
Default submitted-jobs socket.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR peinit2 (8)
